3.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge which is collected is

* $Q = N_{ip}e $ 
* $E_{{\rm dep}} = N_{ip}W$

so that for an air $W/e\simeq33.9\;{\rm  J/C}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

is the energy which is put into the air which is seen by the beam.
For the $0.3\times 6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ beam which is seen at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\times 6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the geometrical length of the chamber taken along the beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times 15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{i}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\,{\rm
cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}  ,
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually traversed by the beam.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot E = I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

and the air kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus is essentially the same,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ beam.  In a scanning geometry a material point
is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beam for

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

where $\Delta s/\Delta t$ is the scan speed.  The number usually quoted
is

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s}\Delta t
       =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/\Delta t}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

i.e. the effective exposure time of a single surface element during
the pass of the $0.3\;{\rm mm}$ spot.  The dose which the element
receives in one energy acquisition is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{t}\,\tau_{d}
      \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
      \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.4\times10^{-3}{\rm rad}\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy
step at the surface.  In air-kerma terms this is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\ {\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}

per pass of the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  For the usual
two-energy K-edge subtraction the surface dose is of course twice this,
$\,\approx 7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scan, $\approx 1.2\times10^{-5}\,
{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy acquisition, i.e. a few $10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}\,
{\rm Gy}$ per full multiple-energy scan, essentially the textbook
$\sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy\,energy^{-1}}$ synchrotron micro-dose.  This is the
conventionally quoted $\dot K_{a}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus, $\,D\simeq\dot K_{a}\tau_{d}$ for the $20\,{\rm ms}$
effective dwell.

*Remark.*  With $W/e=33.97\;{\rm J/C}$ the often quoted number is
$D_{1}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, i.e. $\approx 0.39\,{\rm
mrad\,energy^{-1}}$, essentially negligible for conventional radiobiology,
the $\sim10\!-\!20\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ total being obtained for the $2\!-\!3$
energies normally used in multiple-energy synchrotron imaging.

4.text:
In air the exposure that is recorded by the ionisation chamber can be written
as an air kerma

* $W/e=33.97~{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}\qquad 
m_{air}=\rho_{air} A_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

with the beam area at the focus

$A_{f}=0.30~{\rm mm}\times 6.0~{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm ~cm^{2}}
=1.8\times10^{-6}{\rm ~m^{2}}$

and $L_{c}=15.1~{\rm cm}$ the air length which is traversed by the beam.
Hence

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}A_{f}L_{c}
      =1.293~{\rm kg\,m^{-3}}\times1.8\times10^{-6}{\rm m^{2}}
        \times0.151{\rm m}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}= I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          \approx6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W} .
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}\simeq \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{air}}
       \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \approx1.9\times10^{-4}~{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

per unit mass of air which is actually illuminated.  For a photon beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \Phi\dot\;E\,\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}_{air} ,
\qquad 
\dot D_{t}= \Phi\dot\;E\,\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}_{t}
\end{equation*}

so that with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \dot K_{air}\,
\frac{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}}{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}}
\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}~{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the tissue that is placed at the focus.  In the thin–air limit
$1-\exp\!\big[-(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}\big]\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho
L_{c}$ the same result is obtained as

$$
\dot D_{t}= \frac{I\,W/e}{A_{f}
\bigl[1-\exp(-\mu_{en}\rho L_{c})\bigr]}\;
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
$$

i.e. about $0.19~{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the beam centre.  The beam
dwell time for a surface element which is swept through the $w_{f}=0.30
{\rm mm}$ microbeam is

$$
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}\;,
$$

and by definition $t_{d}=0.02\,{\rm s}$ for the scan used here.  The
surface dose actually received by a material point which is traversed
by the microbeam is therefore

$$
D_{p}= \dot D_{t}\;t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
$$

i.e. $\approx 4~\mu{\rm Gy} \approx 0.4~\mu{\rm rad}$ per beam dwell,
the conventional single-energy, single-pass surface dose at the focus.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\! =\!(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the result is
essentially the air kerma quoted above.  This is the dose which a
surface element receives while the $0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ microbeam
is over it.  The $A_{m}/A_{f}=50/0.3\approx167$ demagnification of the
$50\times6~{\rm mm^{2}}$ monochromator footprint onto the $0.3\times6~{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus gives a mean dose over the whole $50\times6~{\rm mm^{2}}$
scanned field of

$$
\bar D\approx D_{p}\frac{A_{f}}{A_{m}}
      \approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\times\frac{0.3}{50}
      \sim2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm ~Gy},
$$

the usual way in which the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ point dose is quoted as
the average surface dose of the scanned field.  The $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
point value is the one normally quoted as the cumulative surface dose
for the $33.0~{\rm keV}$ single-energy exposure; it scales linearly with
the number of energies used in the multiple-energy imaging sequence.

1.text:
In a parallel plate air chamber the charge collected in the time $dt$ is

\begin{equation*}
dQ = \frac{e}{W}\;dE_{a}
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq33.97~{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air so that the air kerma rate in the
volume which is actually illuminated by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
{\dot K}_{air}=\frac{1}{m_{a}}\frac{dE_{a}}{dt}
          =\frac{W}{e}\,\frac{I}{m_{a}}\;,
\end{equation*}

the $1-g$ factor being $\approx 1$ at 33 keV.  For the focused spot

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}
      =1.8\times 10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the chamber depth taken as the quoted 15.1 cm

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times 15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}} .
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated.  With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{dE_{a}}{dt}= \frac{W}{e}I =33.97\times2.0\times10^{-12}
                 \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
           \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With the usual

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{tissue}\simeq\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{air}
\end{equation*}

the same number is the tissue kerma rate in the illuminated skin element.

A skin element spends in the 0.30 mm focused spot a time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/{\rm exposure}}
     \approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the geometry quoted.  The kerma received by the element which is
actually in the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}\approx\dot K_{air}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.02
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per 20 ms dwell in the
0.3 mm $\times$ 6 mm focused spot, essentially the air kerma rate
$ \approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ quoted above.  This is the peak
surface kerma which a point of skin sees while it is traversed by the
focused beam.  In terms of a mean kerma averaged over the 50 mm
horizontal field which is reconstructed by the scan

\begin{equation*}
\bar K \approx K_{p}\,\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
       \approx K_{p}\,\frac{0.30}{50.0}
       \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ mean surface kerma over the 50 mm $\times$
6 mm field per single energy pass, the $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.006$ duty factor
being the usual scanning-beam averaging.  The 3.9 $\mu$Gy per dwell
is the $ \approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak surface dose which is
normally quoted for the system; with the usual 166$\;\approx\;W_{0}/w_{f}$
simultaneous Laue channels the total $ multi$–energy kerma is
$\approx166\,K_{p}\sim6\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}$ per full scan if the
energies are summed.

2.text:
In air the energy which is transferred to the gas per unit mass is the air
kerma rate

\begin{equation*}
{\dot K}_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{tr}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{1}{m_{a}}\frac{I}{e}W ,
\end{equation*}
$W\simeq 33-34~{\rm eV}$ per ion pair, $e=1.602\times10^{-19}\,$C, so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{tr}=\frac{I}{e}W=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
\,W_{eV}\times1.602\times10^{-19}\approx I\,W_{eV}
\end{equation*}
in eV s$^{-1}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{tr}\approx\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
\times33.97\times1.602\times10^{-19}\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
deposited in the gas which is illuminated by the focus.

The gas mass which is illuminated is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V =\rho_{a}A_{f}L,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm \;cm^{2}},
\qquad L=15.1\;{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V=A_{f}L=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm \;g\,cm^{-3}}V\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}.
\end{equation*}

Hence in air

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\frac{\dot E_{tr}}{m_{a}}
\approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
tissue  kerma rate is the same to a very good approximation,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx \Phi_{E}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}
   \approx\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous air/tissue kerma at the
focus.  In terms of the energy fluence this is

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}= \frac{\dot E_{tr}}{A_{f}\,[1-e^{-\mu\rho L}]}
\approx\frac{\dot E_{tr}}{A_{f}\mu\rho L}\approx5.9\times10^{-7}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
$\mu\rho L\approx0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1\approx6.4\times10^{-3}$,
and $\dot D_{t}=\dot\Phi_{E}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A point on the surface stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$  horizontal
focus for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the quantity quoted as $w_{f}/{\rm step~per~exposure}$.
The dose actually delivered to that point in one pass at 33.0 keV is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.02
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}$ per 33 keV pass,
essentially the air kerma $\approx$ tissue absorbed dose for the
quoted geometry.  This is $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous
tissue kerma at the focus and $\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$
dwell.  For a conventional three energy multiple-energy set the
cumulative point dose is $\approx3D_{1}\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}$ Gy
per raster, i.e. of order ten microgray per imaging scan, the usual
synchrotron micro-beam imaging regime.  With the customary $W=34\,{\rm eV}$
the often quoted number is $D_{1}\approx4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx0.2\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ dose-rate at
the focus.

