          =\frac{W}{e}\,\frac{I}{m_{a}}\;,
\end{equation*}

the $1-g$ factor being $\approx 1$ at 33 keV.  For the focused spot

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}
      =1.8\times 10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the chamber depth taken as the quoted 15.1 cm

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times 15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}} .
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated.  With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{dE_{a}}{dt}= \frac{W}{e}I =33.97\times2.0\times10^{-12}
                 \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
           \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With the usual

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{tissue}\simeq\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{air}
\end{equation*}

the same number is the tissue kerma rate in the illuminated skin element.

A skin element spends in the 0.30 mm focused spot a time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/{\rm exposure}}
     \approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the geometry quoted.  The kerma received by the element which is
actually in the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}\approx\dot K_{air}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.02
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per 20 ms dwell in the
0.3 mm $\times$ 6 mm focused spot, essentially the air kerma rate
$ \approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ quoted above.  This is the peak
surface kerma which a point of skin sees while it is traversed by the
focused beam.  In terms of a mean kerma averaged over the 50 mm
horizontal field which is reconstructed by the scan

\begin{equation*}
\bar K \approx K_{p}\,\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
       \approx K_{p}\,\frac{0.30}{50.0}
       \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ mean surface kerma over the 50 mm $\times$
6 mm field per single energy pass, the $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.006$ duty factor
being the usual scanning-beam averaging.  The 3.9 $\mu$Gy per dwell
is the $ \approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak surface dose which is
normally quoted for the system; with the usual 166$\;\approx\;W_{0}/w_{f}$
simultaneous Laue channels the total $ multi$–energy kerma is
$\approx166\,K_{p}\sim6\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}$ per full scan if the
energies are summed.

public_text.2:
In air the energy which is transferred to the gas per unit mass is the air
kerma rate

\begin{equation*}
{\dot K}_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{tr}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{1}{m_{a}}\frac{I}{e}W ,
\end{equation*}
$W\simeq 33-34~{\rm eV}$ per ion pair, $e=1.602\times10^{-19}\,$C, so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{tr}=\frac{I}{e}W=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
\,W_{eV}\times1.602\times10^{-19}\approx I\,W_{eV}
\end{equation*}
in eV s$^{-1}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{tr}\approx\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}}{1.602\times10^{-19}}
\times33.97\times1.602\times10^{-19}\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
deposited in the gas which is illuminated by the focus.

The gas mass which is illuminated is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V =\rho_{a}A_{f}L,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm \;cm^{2}},
\qquad L=15.1\;{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V=A_{f}L=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm \;g\,cm^{-3}}V\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}.
\end{equation*}

Hence in air

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\frac{\dot E_{tr}}{m_{a}}
\approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
tissue  kerma rate is the same to a very good approximation,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx \Phi_{E}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}
   \approx\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous air/tissue kerma at the
focus.  In terms of the energy fluence this is

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}= \frac{\dot E_{tr}}{A_{f}\,[1-e^{-\mu\rho L}]}
\approx\frac{\dot E_{tr}}{A_{f}\mu\rho L}\approx5.9\times10^{-7}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
$\mu\rho L\approx0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1\approx6.4\times10^{-3}$,
and $\dot D_{t}=\dot\Phi_{E}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A point on the surface stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$  horizontal
focus for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the quantity quoted as $w_{f}/{\rm step~per~exposure}$.
The dose actually delivered to that point in one pass at 33.0 keV is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.02
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}$ per 33 keV pass,
essentially the air kerma $\approx$ tissue absorbed dose for the
quoted geometry.  This is $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous
tissue kerma at the focus and $\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$
dwell.  For a conventional three energy multiple-energy set the
cumulative point dose is $\approx3D_{1}\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}$ Gy
per raster, i.e. of order ten microgray per imaging scan, the usual
synchrotron micro-beam imaging regime.  With the customary $W=34\,{\rm eV}$
the often quoted number is $D_{1}\approx4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx0.2\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ dose-rate at
the focus.

1.text:
In the volume of air which is actually illuminated at the focus the
absorbed energy rate is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \frac{W}{e}I \approx 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}\times
2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air.  The mass of air which is in
the beam at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the illuminated volume is

\begin{equation*}
 \dot K_{air}= \frac{1}{m_{a}}\frac{dE_{a}}{dt}
           =\frac{W}{e}\frac{I}{m_{a}}
           \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\Bigr)_{tissue}\simeq\Bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\Bigr)_{air}
\end{equation*}

the tissue kerma, essentially the absorbed dose, at the focus is the
same

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
            \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in the $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ beam.  In the thin-air limit

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\frac{I\,W/e}
{A_{f}\bigl[1-e^{-(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}}\bigr]}
\Bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\Bigr)_{t}
\end{equation*}

gives the same number, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$.

A surface element spends in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ micro-beam the
dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\approx0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the quoted $w_{f}/{\rm step\,per\,exposure}$.  The kerma actually
delivered to a point which is traversed by the focused spot in one
energy pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}\simeq\dot K_{air}t_{d}\simeq
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}\approx0.39\times10^{-3}{\rm rad}
\end{equation*}

per $33.0\;{\rm keV}$, $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell at the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
this is the surface absorbed dose to tissue in the micro-beam,

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous at the
focus.  For the usual two energy K-edge subtraction this is
$\approx2D_{1}\sim7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per raster and
$\approx3D_{1}\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\;{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy
acquisition, the textbook $\sim10^{-5}\;{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron
micro-dose.

Field averaged over the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator footprint
the duty factor is $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.30/50=6.0\times10^{-3}$, so that the
mean surface kerma over the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ scanned field
per energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
\bar K\approx K_{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
      \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\times6\times10^{-3}
      \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\ {\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per single $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ pass, $\approx0.04\!-\!0.06\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for a two energy set, the commonly quoted mean surface dose of the
scanned area.  The $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak point value is the
conventionally quoted cumulative surface dose for the $33.0\;{\rm keV}$
single-energy dwell; it scales linearly with the number of energies
summed in the multiple-energy sequence.  This is essentially the
$0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous tissue kerma at the focus. ∎

2.text:
In air the ionisation current is

$dE_{a}/dt = I\,W/e$, $\;W/e \simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

so that in the gas which is actually illuminated by the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
       \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

is deposited per second.

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air in the beam.  Hence the air kerma rate in the
illuminated volume is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ at
$33.0\;{\rm keV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the instantaneous tissue kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
microbeam.  In energy fluence form

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}\simeq\frac{\dot E_{a}}{A_{f}\mu\rho L_{c}}
          \approx5\!-\!6\times10^{-7}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},\qquad
\mu\rho L_{c}\approx0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1\approx6.4\times10^{-3},
\end{equation*}

and $\dot D_{t}=\dot\Phi_{E}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
microbeam for the quoted dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma actually received by a point which is traversed by
the spot in one energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
D_{p}\simeq\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
      \approx0.39\ {\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}

per $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ dwell in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus,
i.e. the familiar $\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ synchrotron micro-beam
rate.  This is the peak surface kerma which a skin element sees while
it is in the beam.

Averaged over the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which is
reconstructed by the scan the duty factor is $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.30/50=6.0\times
10^{-3}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bar K\simeq D_{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}\approx
4\times10^{-6}\times6\times10^{-3}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx20\!-\!30\ {\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

mean surface kerma over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per
single-energy pass.  With the usual two-energy K edge subtraction the
point dose is $ \approx 2D_{p}\sim7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per raster,
$\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy acquisition,
essentially the textbook $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy\,energy^{-1}}$ synchrotron
micro-dose, negligible on a conventional radiobiological scale.  The
$ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell is normally quoted as
the cumulative surface dose at the focus; the field mean is $w_{f}/W_{0}$
smaller, $\sim 2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy.

3.text:
With $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air the power which is put into
the air which is seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is actually illuminated at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate in the illuminated mass is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ 
{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$1-g\simeq1$ at 33 keV.  With

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{t}\simeq\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{air}
\end{equation*}

the same number is the tissue kerma / absorbed dose rate in the
$0.30\times6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}$ micro-beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus, i.e. $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous tissue
kerma in the 33.0 keV beam.

A material point on the surface is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal micro-beam for the quoted effective dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma actually received by the point while the spot is
over it in one energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}= \dot K_{a} t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.39\!-\!0.40\;{\rm mrad}$ per 33 keV dwell,
$K_{p}\simeq\dot D_{t}t_{d}$.  This is the conventionally quoted
peak surface kerma / absorbed dose for the synchrotron micro-beam.
In air-kerma terms $K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$
pass of the $0.3\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

Mean kerma averaged over the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which
is reconstructed by the scan is obtained with the scanning duty factor
$w_{f}/W_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\bar K_{1}\approx K_{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
      \approx3.8\times10^{-6}\frac{0.30}{50.0}
      \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per single energy pass per unit area of the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
reconstructed field.  The $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous rate
and the $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell are the numbers
normally quoted for the system.

The dose scales linearly with the number $N_{E}$ of energies used in the
multiple-energy sequence.  For the usual two–energy K-edge subtraction
$N_{E}=2$,

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{2E}\approx2K_{p}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.8\;{\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}

peak per raster, $\bar K^{2E}\approx4\!-\!6\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ mean
over the field, and for a nominal three-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{3E}\approx3K_{p}\approx1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

peak per point, $\approx6\!-\!9\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ mean over the
$50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field, i.e. of order ten microgray per full
multiple-energy scan, essentially the textbook $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy\;energy^{-1}}$
synchrotron micro-dose.  The $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous, quoted above is the $N_{E}=1$ $33\;{\rm keV}$ value. ∎

