      \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\ {\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per single $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ pass, $\approx0.04\!-\!0.06\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for a two energy set, the commonly quoted mean surface dose of the
scanned area.  The $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak point value is the
conventionally quoted cumulative surface dose for the $33.0\;{\rm keV}$
single-energy dwell; it scales linearly with the number of energies
summed in the multiple-energy sequence.  This is essentially the
$0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous tissue kerma at the focus. ∎

public_text.2:
In air the ionisation current is

$dE_{a}/dt = I\,W/e$, $\;W/e \simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

so that in the gas which is actually illuminated by the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
       \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

is deposited per second.

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air in the beam.  Hence the air kerma rate in the
illuminated volume is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ at
$33.0\;{\rm keV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the instantaneous tissue kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
microbeam.  In energy fluence form

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}\simeq\frac{\dot E_{a}}{A_{f}\mu\rho L_{c}}
          \approx5\!-\!6\times10^{-7}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},\qquad
\mu\rho L_{c}\approx0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1\approx6.4\times10^{-3},
\end{equation*}

and $\dot D_{t}=\dot\Phi_{E}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
microbeam for the quoted dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma actually received by a point which is traversed by
the spot in one energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
D_{p}\simeq\dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
      \approx0.39\ {\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}

per $33.0\;{\rm keV}$ dwell in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus,
i.e. the familiar $\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ synchrotron micro-beam
rate.  This is the peak surface kerma which a skin element sees while
it is in the beam.

Averaged over the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which is
reconstructed by the scan the duty factor is $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.30/50=6.0\times
10^{-3}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bar K\simeq D_{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}\approx
4\times10^{-6}\times6\times10^{-3}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx20\!-\!30\ {\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

mean surface kerma over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per
single-energy pass.  With the usual two-energy K edge subtraction the
point dose is $ \approx 2D_{p}\sim7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per raster,
$\sim1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy acquisition,
essentially the textbook $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy\,energy^{-1}}$ synchrotron
micro-dose, negligible on a conventional radiobiological scale.  The
$ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell is normally quoted as
the cumulative surface dose at the focus; the field mean is $w_{f}/W_{0}$
smaller, $\sim 2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy.

public_text.3:
With $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air the power which is put into
the air which is seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is actually illuminated at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate in the illuminated mass is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ 
{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$1-g\simeq1$ at 33 keV.  With

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{t}\simeq\bigl(\mu_{en}/\rho\bigr)_{air}
\end{equation*}

the same number is the tissue kerma / absorbed dose rate in the
$0.30\times6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}$ micro-beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus, i.e. $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous tissue
kerma in the 33.0 keV beam.

A material point on the surface is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal micro-beam for the quoted effective dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma actually received by the point while the spot is
over it in one energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}= \dot K_{a} t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.39\!-\!0.40\;{\rm mrad}$ per 33 keV dwell,
$K_{p}\simeq\dot D_{t}t_{d}$.  This is the conventionally quoted
peak surface kerma / absorbed dose for the synchrotron micro-beam.
In air-kerma terms $K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$
pass of the $0.3\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

Mean kerma averaged over the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which
is reconstructed by the scan is obtained with the scanning duty factor
$w_{f}/W_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\bar K_{1}\approx K_{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
      \approx3.8\times10^{-6}\frac{0.30}{50.0}
      \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per single energy pass per unit area of the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
reconstructed field.  The $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous rate
and the $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell are the numbers
normally quoted for the system.

The dose scales linearly with the number $N_{E}$ of energies used in the
multiple-energy sequence.  For the usual two–energy K-edge subtraction
$N_{E}=2$,

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{2E}\approx2K_{p}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.8\;{\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}

peak per raster, $\bar K^{2E}\approx4\!-\!6\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ mean
over the field, and for a nominal three-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{3E}\approx3K_{p}\approx1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

peak per point, $\approx6\!-\!9\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ mean over the
$50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field, i.e. of order ten microgray per full
multiple-energy scan, essentially the textbook $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy\;energy^{-1}}$
synchrotron micro-dose.  The $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous, quoted above is the $N_{E}=1$ $33\;{\rm keV}$ value. ∎

2.text:
In the thin chamber limit $\mu_{en}\rho L_{c}\ll1$ the charge collected is the
energy put into the air which is seen by the focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e ,\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;
{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ micro beam. 

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;
{\rm cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{a}=A_{f}L_{c}=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\;
{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}=0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1
\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that $1-g\simeq1$ and the air kerma rate in the illuminated focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \dot E_{a}/m_{a}= I\,W/e/m_{a}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ at 33.0 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is the instantaneous tissue kerma/absorbed dose rate in the centre of the
focused micro-beam, $\dot\Phi_{E}(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}$ with
$\dot\Phi_{E}\approx5\!-\!6\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$.

A surface element stays in the horizontal $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ spot for the
effective dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the $w_{f}$/scan-step per exposure time quoted in the data, so that the
peak kerma actually received while the spot is over the point in one
33.0 keV pass is the conventional synchrotron micro-beam surface dose

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{(1)}\simeq\dot D_{t}t_{d}
   \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
   \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
   \approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}
\end{equation*}
per 33 keV, 20 ms dwell in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.
$K_{p}^{(1)}=\dot K_{air}t_{d}$ is the $N_{E}=1$ textbook value,
$0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous at the focus.

On a field average the spot is rastered over the monochromator footprint
$W_{0}=50\,{\rm mm}$.  The scanning duty factor is

\begin{equation*}
{\rm duty}=w_{f}/W_{0}=0.30/50=6.0\times10^{-3},
\end{equation*}
so that the mean kerma averaged over the reconstructed
$W_{0}\times6\,{\rm mm}=50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field per single energy
raster is

\begin{equation*}
\bar K_{1}\simeq K_{p}^{(1)}\,w_{f}/W_{0}
       \approx4.0\times10^{-6}\times6.0\times10^{-3}
       \approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\;{\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}
per 33 keV pass, $\approx24\,{\rm nGy\,energy^{-1}}$ duty-factor mean.

The dose scales linearly with the number $N_{E}$ of energies summed in the
multiple-energy sequence,

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{N_{E}}=N_{E}K_{p}^{(1)},\qquad
\bar K^{N_{E}}=N_{E}\bar K_{1}.
\end{equation*}

For the usual two energy K-edge subtraction $N_{E}=2$

\begin{equation*}
K_{p}^{2E}\approx2K_{p}^{(1)}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
peak point per raster, $\approx0.76\;{\rm mrad}$ peak, and

\begin{equation*}
\bar K^{2E}\approx2\bar K_{1}\approx4\!-\!6\times10^{-8}\;{\rm Gy}
\approx40\!-\!60\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}
mean over the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field, $ \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous at the focus.  With a nominal three energy acquisition
$K_{p}^{3E}\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak point and
$\bar K^{3E}\approx60\!-\!90\;{\rm nGy}$ field mean.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm
Gy\,energy^{-1}}$ peak point, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous,
$\sim24\;{\rm nGy\,energy^{-1}}$ duty averaged value is the $N_{E}=1$
33 keV synchrotron micro-beam surface dose normally quoted; ~ $7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
peak, $\sim 50\;{\rm nGy}$ mean over the field, is the conventional
two-energy multiple energy imaging surface dose. ∎

